#N canvas 40 60 1280 1200 12;
#X declare -path ..;
#X obj 0 0 cnv 1 1280 46 empty empty example\ \ \ the\ applause\ drummer\ \ -\ \ reinforcement\ learning\ from\ your\ claps\ (REINFORCE\,\ Williams\ 1992) 14 23 0 20 #1d3557 #ffffff 0;
#X obj 1110 1170 declare -path ..;
#X text 800 1170 finds the nn.* objects one folder up ->, f 42;
#X text 20 1170 neuralplay - Moisés Horta Valenzuela / SEMILLA.AI Studio, f 70;
#X text 20 56 Four firing neurons - kick \, snare \, hat \, rim - learn a groove from applause. On every 16th note each one fires with a probability: its POLICY. Clap (space bar) when you like what you hear \, boo (b) when you do not \, and what you just heard becomes more \, or less \, likely. Nobody ever tells it the pattern., f 175;
#X obj 10 96 cnv 1 390 640 empty empty THE\ IDEA:\ learning\ what\ to\ do 8 12 0 13 #eef1f5 #1d3557 0;
#X text 20 122 POLICY LEARNING. Lesson 17 learned VALUES (how good is each note?) and then picked the best. This drummer learns what to DO \, directly. Each of its 4 neurons has one weight theta for each of the 16 steps of a bar and FIRES with probability p = sigmoid(theta / T): a stochastic neuron. Firing at random is its exploration., f 52;
#X text 20 233 REINFORCE (Ronald Williams \, 1992): after a reward \, move each weight by rate * reward * (x - p) / T. A rewarded hit (x = 1) becomes more likely \, a rewarded rest (x = 0) makes the hit less likely. (x - p) / T is the slope of the log-probability of what the neuron did., f 52;
#X text 20 329 CREDIT \, AGAIN: a clap comes late and is about the last moment \, not one 16th note. So each neuron keeps a fading MEMORY of what it did \, the eligibility trace e <- lambda * e + (x - p) (Barto \, Sutton & Anderson \, 1983). A clap moves theta by rate * reward * e / T: the hits you just heard the most \, those of a bar ago hardly at all., f 52;
#X text 20 455 THE DANCER is an automatic critic for tired hands. After every 16th note it pays r = the average over the drums of (2x - 1)(2 taste - 1): +1 for doing what it likes \, -1 for the opposite. The drummer only gets that number \, never the taste graphs. Draw a new taste and the policy follows., f 52;
#X text 20 566 TEMPERATURE T: high = adventurous (p closer to 1/2) \, low = sure of itself. Even a learned groove varies a little from bar to bar - a drummer \, not a sequencer., f 52;
#X text 20 632 THE BIG VERSION is RLHF: a network acts \, people rate it \, and updates of this kind (PPO \, a careful REINFORCE) make the liked behaviour more likely., f 52;
#X obj 10 746 cnv 1 390 420 empty empty TRY\ THIS 8 12 0 13 #e6f6e9 #1d3557 0;
#X text 20 772 1) Raise the volume: every drum fires half of the time - noise. Switch the dancer on: within 20 to 30 bars a house groove appears in the policy graphs (or press practice)., f 52;
#X text 20 838 2) Click clave or breakbeat \, or draw a taste of your own with the mouse: the policy follows a reward it cannot see., f 52;
#X text 20 889 3) Dancer off \, forget. Now YOU are the critic: space bar right after something you like \, b after a mess. A few minutes of clapping teach it your groove., f 52;
#X text 20 955 4) Lambda 0: only the last 16th gets the credit \, and your late claps reward the wrong hits. Lambda 0.9: one clap pays the whole last half bar., f 52;
#X text 20 1021 5) Rate 3 with the dancer: fast \, but a neuron can lock at p = 0 by mistake - it never fires again \, so it never finds out., f 52;
#X obj 410 96 cnv 1 860 430 empty empty THE\ GROOVE\ \ (16\ steps\ =\ one\ bar\,\ four\ drums) 8 12 0 13 #e3f0fb #1d3557 0;
#X text 466 122 THE DANCER'S TASTE: the drummer never sees it. Draw on it!, f 34;
#X text 726 122 THE POLICY: the chance of each hit \, p, f 34;
#X text 986 122 THE MEMORY e: what a clap pays now (+ hit \, - rest), f 34;
